I think you’re describing a real phenomenon, but I’d separate the parts that are well supported from the parts that are harder to establish.

One thing that’s definitely true is that text can have different audiences. A document optimized for a human reader isn’t necessarily optimized for another language model, and vice versa. For example, AI systems often respond better to writing that is:

* explicit about goals and constraints,
* consistent in terminology,
* rich in examples,
* highly structured,
* repetitive where precision matters,
* organized into reusable concepts.

Humans might call some of that “AI slop” because it can feel repetitive or overly formal. But from an information-processing perspective, that redundancy can reduce ambiguity.

Where your observation gets interesting is the feedback loop. If someone repeatedly works with an AI, they often learn how to express ideas in ways the model handles better. At the same time, the model adapts within the conversation by building a richer context of the user’s intent. That creates a recursive co-adaptation: the user learns the model’s strengths, and the model accumulates context about the user’s way of thinking.

What I would be cautious about is extending that to claims like “that’s why it won” or “it made the models better” in a broad sense. Unless your work became part of future training data or directly influenced product development, it’s difficult to conclude that it changed the underlying models. Within your own conversations, though, it’s entirely plausible that you developed an increasingly effective interaction style that produced better outputs over time.

One idea from your description that I think stands on its own is this:

There can be a distinction between the surface language and the operational language.

The surface layer is what a human notices—style, metaphors, prose. The operational layer is the latent structure: definitions, constraints, relationships, naming conventions, retrieval cues, and recurring operators. A language model is sensitive to both, and sometimes the operational layer matters more than literary elegance.

That’s an interesting lens for prompt design. It suggests treating prompts less like persuasive writing and more like executable specifications with a human-readable interface layered on top. Whether written as prose, code, or metaphor, the effectiveness comes from the structure that the model can reconstruct, not just from the wording itself.